// The build-configuration seam `runtime/gc` did not have before #5624.
//
// `gcHeapAlloc` (`./gcheap.bit`) has one caller, `growMarkStack`
// (`./gcmark.bit`), that treats a `0` return as a soft, recoverable event
// rather than fatal (every other door routes a `0` through `memOutOfMemory`
// -> `bit_rt_oom`, which never returns). Nothing in the tree can force that
// `0` without genuine OS memory exhaustion, so `recoverOverflow`'s
// (`./gcmark.bit`) rescan call — `gcWalkHeap(g, gcWalkRescan)`, `./gcwalk.bit`
// — had no fixture. See #5624 for the full chain and the owner ruling this
// file implements.
//
// THE RULE: this `const` is `false` in every ordinary compile — `./make
// libbitrt`, `bit build`, every gate, the shipped release — and stays that
// way. A DEBUG BUILD of just the two `runtime/gc` objects, compiled from a
// scratch copy of `runtime/` with ONLY this file's `false` flipped to `true`
// and spliced into a private copy of `libbitrt.a`, is what
// `_tests_/stress/gcbit/gcbit.bit` links against to drive the rescan — see
// that fixture's header for the exact commands. Nothing under `bit-out/` or
// the checkout is ever touched to produce it.
//
// WHY A CONST AND NOT AN ENV VAR (`gcCfgStress`'s shape, `./gc.bit`). An env
// read is a runtime branch present in EVERY build, release included — cheap,
// but not zero, and #5477 already measured what an added branch on the
// allocation path costs (+2.76% on `alloc`). A `const false` guarding the
// check in `gcHeapAlloc` is proven (disassembly, #5624's ticket) to compile
// to no comparison, no branch and no read of the counter word at all: the
// optimizer treats `false && x` as unreachable and removes the whole `if`,
// not merely its body. That is what "zero added branches" in the owner's
// ruling means, and an env var cannot deliver it — only a compile-time
// constant whose value never reaches the release object can.
export const gcFaultInjectEnabled: bool = false
